Z||000005||PBJ||08-11-2001||09:20 PM||||WOW,   This is a huge question. lets narrow it down.<P>how much money?<BR>are you doing the work?<BR>skill level?<BR>what do you want to end up with. ie 4-6-8 cyl?<BR>time your car can be "laid up for"?<P>I like engine swaps!||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||08-11-2001||10:04 PM||||You'll be better off getting another Duke. If you want more HP, get another Fiero with a V6...it'll end up being cheaper.<P>The Duke was used in a lot of GM cars, both compact and intermediate. You'll be able to find one cheap, but check it out good before you commit yourself.<P>I really don't know why everyone supposedly hates the Dukes so much. Mine, with auto and air, gets 36 mpg. While it isn't a race car, it's quick off the line. And the maintenance is a lot easier in that cramped engine bay than a V6 or a V8 would be.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deceler8_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||08-11-2001||11:13 PM||||This dude made up his mind before this thread started, he's just looking for confirmation.<P>Whatever you decided you want, go for it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Pilrulz||08-12-2001||05:21 PM||||you just happen to be right. i am planning to tear into the engine, and probably end up rebuilding it unless i find a cheap engine that runs good.  basically, the head gasket went bad suddenly and got a whole bunch of water and antifreeze into the oil, which made it sludge up like liquified duct tape.  the previous owner just didn't want to mess with it so i got a really good deal.  also, the first three cars we bought after i was born had the venerable duke underhood, so i've spent a lot of time with them.<P>                               pil ||||reg||12
Z||000009||roadwarrior||08-12-2001||05:35 PM||||if your oil and anti freeze mixed you might wanna pull your engine and check/replace all your bearings<P>Mitch||||reg||1
Z||000010||qwikgta||08-12-2001||06:29 PM||||Seeing that you are a new member try the search function located next to the "post a reply" button, look for engine swaps, there is so much #$%^ info on this forum we could be here all week going over it with you.  Here are some pointers.<BR> 1. Pick the amount of money you want to spend on the project.<BR> 2. Get the complete donor car, you will need many parts, do not get a piece here and a piece there.<BR> 3. Get some more money<BR> 4. Get many friends to help you.  Find a local Fiero club in your area, or at least a POCI club (Pontiac Oakland Club Int)<BR> 5. Attend a fiero car show and look over the engine swaps, talk to the driver, find out who did the work and ask questions.<BR> 6. Get some more money.<BR> 7. Check out ACE, V8 Archie, Chris West, Bubbajoexx and Ed Parks Sites for more info.<P>Good luck<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://www.homestead.com/robsfieroproject   <BR>http://www.vafieros.com<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Avery Montembeault||08-13-2001||01:16 PM||||I have an 88 iron duke, 87,000 on it, lots of new parts, $600 if you're interested. Reccently passed inspection.||||reg||1
